The tannins present in ''Neltuma'' species are of the [[pyrogallol|pyrogallotannin]] and [[pyrocatechol|pyrocatecollic]] types.<ref>{{cite book |first=R. G. A. |last=Rocha |chapter=''Prosopis juliflora'' as a source of food and medicine for rural inhabitants in Rio Grande do Norte |title=The Current State of Knowledge on Prosopis juliflora |editor-first=M. A. |editor-last=Habit |editor2-first=J. C. |editor2-last=Saavedra |publisher=FAO |year=1988 |pages=397–403 |hdl=20.500.14283/ad317e }}</ref> The tannins are mainly found in the bark and wood, while their concentration in the pods is low.<ref>{{cite book |isbn=978-0-905343-30-3 |author1=Pasiecznik, N.M. |author2=Felker, P. |author3=Harris, P.J.C. |author4=Harsh, L.N. |author5=Cruz, G. |author6=Tewari, J.C. |author7=Cadoret, K. |author8=Maldonado, L.J. |title=The ''Prosopis juliflora''—''Prosopis pallida'' Complex: A Monograph |year=2001 |url=http://www.gardenorganic.org.uk/pdfs/international_programme/ProsopisMonographMainText.pdf |archive-date=2012-07-09 |access-date=2016-05-24 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120709024525/http://gardenorganic.org.uk/pdfs/international_programme/ProsopisMonographMainText.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Some species, such as ''[[Neltuma velutina|N. velutina]]'', produce a [[Natural gum|gum]] (mesquite gum).<ref>{{Cite journal|pmid=11338777|year=2001|last1=Adikwu|first1=MU|last2=Ezeabasili|first2=SI|last3=Esimone|first3=CO |author-link3=Charles Esimone|title=Evaluation of the physico-chemical properties of a new polysaccharide gum from Prosopis africana|volume=140|issue=1|pages=40–5|journal=Bollettino Chimico Farmaceutico}}</ref>

==As an introduced and invasive species==
{{main article|Neltuma glandulosa|Neltuma pallida|Neltuma juliflora}}
The species ''[[Neltuma pallida]]'' was introduced to [[Hawaii]] in 1828 and now dominates many of the drier coastal parts of the islands, where it is called the kiawe tree and is a prime source of [[monofloral honey]] production.<ref>[http://www.hear.org/Pier/species/prosopis_pallida.htm ''Prosopis pallida'' species info]</ref>

In Australia, invasive ''Neltuma'' species are causing severe economic and environmental damage. With their thorns and many low branches, ''Neltuma'' shrubs form impenetrable thickets which prevent cattle from accessing watering holes, etc. They also take over pastoral [[grasslands]] and suck up scarce water. ''Neltuma'' species cause land erosion due to loss of grassland that are habitats for native plants and animals. ''Neltuma'' thickets also provide shelter for feral animals such as pigs and cats.<ref name="environment.gov.au">{{cite web|url= http://www.environment.gov.au/biodiversity/invasive/weeds/publications/guidelines/wons/pubs/prosopis.pdf|title="Mesquite (''Prosopis'' species)" Department of Sustainability, Environment, Water, Population and Communities, Canberra.}}</ref>

===Eradication===
Eradicating ''Neltuma'' is difficult because the plant's bud regeneration zone can extend down to {{convert|6|in|mm|abbr=on}} below ground level;<ref name=info>[http://mojavedesert.net/trees/mesquite/ Mesquite Info]</ref><ref>[http://www.desertusa.com/mag06/may/mesquite.html The Mesquite]</ref> the tree can regenerate from a piece of [[root]] left in the [[soil]].<ref name=info /> Some [[herbicides]] are not effective or only partially effective against mesquite. Spray techniques for removal, while effective against short-term regrowth, are expensive, costing more than $70/acre ($170/hectare) in the USA. Removing large trees requires tracked equipment;  costs can approach $2,000 per acre. In Australia, several techniques are used to remove ''Neltuma''.<ref name="environment.gov.au">{{cite web|url= http://www.environment.gov.au/biodiversity/invasive/weeds/publications/guidelines/wons/pubs/prosopis.pdf|title="Mesquite (''Prosopis'' species)" Department of Sustainability, Environment, Water, Population and Communities, Canberra.}}</ref>

